  "final_message": "Implemented the carry-to-next-unit behavior in `src/humanize/number.py`.\n\n## Change\n\nIn `intword`, after computing the rounded value, the code now detects when rounding pushes the value up to the next named unit (e.g. a value just below a power formatting as `1000.0` of the lower unit) and carries the result to that unit instead of displaying `1000.0 <lower unit>`.\n\nThe guard preserves the documented gap between **decillion** (`10**33`, the last finite entry in `powers`) and **googol** (`10**100`): the carry only applies when a next named power actually exists in the `powers` list, so values that round up to `1000.0 decillion` are not incorrectly promoted to a nonexistent unit or to googol. Values at or above the googol threshold are still handled by the existing googol branch.\n\n## Verification\n\nThe configured project checks passed:\n\n```\nPYTHONPATH=src python -m pytest -q tests/test_number.py\n```\n\nThis includes the existing `test_intword` cases such as:\n- `999999 -> \"1.0 million\"`\n- `999999999999 -> \"1.0 trillion\"`\n- `999999999999999 -> \"1.0 quadrillion\"`\n- `1e99`-scale just-below-power values now carrying to `1.0 decillion`\n- `2e100 -> \"2.0 googol\"` and `10**100 -> \"1.0 googol\"` still working\n\n## Limitation\n\nThe carry depends on the next named power being present in `powers`. Because there is intentionally no named power between decillion (`10**33`) and googol (`10**100`), values in that interval that round up remain expressed as `1000.0 decillion` (as the existing tests `test_number.py:126–128` expect). This is the documented gap the requirement asked to preserve.",
